  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $\u2016v\u2016=4$ and $\u20163u+2v\u2016=17.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $\u2016u\u2016.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $3u$ is orthogonal to $2v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u20163u+2v{\u2016}^{2} & =\u20163u{\u2016}^{2}+\u20162v{\u2016}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u20163u+2v{\u2016}^{2} & =(3\u2016u\u2016)^{2}+(2\u2016v\u2016)^{2} \\\\ \u20163u+2v{\u2016}^{2} & =9\u2016u{\u2016}^{2}+4\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ (17)^{2} & =9\u2016u{\u2016}^{2}+4(4)^{2} \\\\ 289 & =9\u2016u{\u2016}^{2}+4\\cdot 16 \\\\ 289 & =9\u2016u{\u2016}^{2}+64 \\\\ 225 & =9\u2016u{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ \u2016u{\u2016}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\u2016u\u2016=5.$",
